<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/social-psychology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Social psychology measures <strong>how the presence of others — real, imagined or implied —
    changes behaviour</strong>. Its founding assumption, the <strong>social approach</strong>,
    is that behaviour cannot be understood by studying the individual in isolation: the
    situation is a genuine cause, not background scenery. Its methods follow the claim —
    experiments that manipulate social pressure directly (an obedient voice, an unanimous
    wrong answer, a collapsing stranger on a real subway) and measure what real people
    actually do, in laboratories and in the field. Its findings are consistently humbling:
    ordinary situations move ordinary people further than personality does, and our
    self-narratives rationalise behaviour more often than they cause it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="situational power, from obedience to dissonance" widget="equationStep" config={socialCfg} />

  <h2>Milgram: obedience measured in volts</h2>
  <p>
    The design (1961-63, Yale): 40 men aged 20-50 respond to a "memory and learning" ad; a
    stern experimenter in a lab coat instructs them, as "teacher", to shock a "learner" in
    escalating 15-volt steps to a maximum of 450, through a panel the learner cannot see.
    The learner (an actor) pounds the wall at 300 V and falls silent. The clinical
    instruction sequence — "please continue", "the experiment requires that you continue" —
    is the point: nothing forces the teacher except structured social pressure. The result:
    <strong>65% went to 450 V, and none stopped before 300</strong> — while psychiatrists
    polled in advance predicted fewer than 1% would. Milgram's variations map the pressure
    onto distance and dissent: same-room proximity dropped full obedience to about 40%,
    orders by telephone to about 22.5%, a shabby downtown office to about 48%, and
    introducing a second authority who said "stop" collapsed it towards zero. His
    <strong>agency theory</strong> reads the pattern: under legitimate authority people
    shift into an agentic state — "I was following orders" — outsourcing moral
    responsibility to the commander.
  </p>
  <p>
    The study carries two permanent lessons beyond obedience itself. Methodologically: the
    prediction error is the finding — we systematically misjudge situational power because
    we do dispositions for others (see attribution below). Ethically: the deception and
    visible distress of participants triggered modern ethics review, informed consent and
    debriefing norms — a science rebuilt, partly, on this experiment's controversy. The
    finding itself survived: Burger's 2009 partial replication (halted at 150 V) matched
    Milgram's compliance rate almost exactly, and cross-cultural versions from Munich to
    cross-national meta-analyses find the obedience gradient robust.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Milgram shows people are evil. It shows the opposite:
    the participants were ordinary, protested, sweated and trembled — and obeyed anyway.
    The explanation is structural (authority, graduated commitment, diffusion of
    responsibility to the experimenter), which is why "bad apples" explanations of
    atrocities keep missing the barrel.</p>

  <h2>Conformity and its brakes</h2>
  <p>
    Sherif's autokinetic studies (1935) showed norms being <em>born</em>: in an ambiguous
    situation (a stationary light that appears to move), individuals' estimates converge
    into a group standard. Asch then asked the sharper question: what if the majority is
    <em>obviously</em> wrong? In his line-length judgments (1951), one real participant
    answered alongside 6-8 confederates who unanimously picked a plainly wrong line: about
    75% conformed at least once, on roughly a third of critical trials — against an error
    rate near 1% when answering alone. Post-experiment interviews are the data's soul:
    some believed the majority (perception distorted), most knew better and went along
    anyway (normative pressure). The brakes, found by varying the setup: <strong>unanimity
    is load-bearing</strong> — a single dissenting ally drops conformity by around
    three-quarters; private written answers reduce it; so does having answered publicly
    once before. A modern meta-analysis of 133 replications (Bond & Smith, 1996) found
    conformity stronger in collectivist cultures — the effect is social machinery, tuned
    by social values.
  </p>

  <h2>Attribution: the stories we tell about why</h2>
  <p>
    Heider founded the field by noticing that people are naive psychologists, explaining
    behaviour as internal (dispositional) or external (situational). Kelley's covariation
    model formalises the ideal calculation: does the person behave this way only with this
    target (<em>distinctiveness</em>), only in this situation (<em>consistency</em>), and
    do most people behave this way here (<em>consensus</em>)? Real attributions take
    shortcuts, and the master shortcut is the <strong>fundamental attribution error</strong>
    (Ross, 1977): overweighting disposition, underweighting situation. Jones and Harris
    showed it with essays: readers who <em>knew</em> the writer had been assigned a
    pro-Castro position still rated the writer as more pro-Castro than anti-Castro
    essayists. Two variants complete the set: the <strong>actor-observer asymmetry</strong>
    (I cut the lights because it's foggy; you cut them because you're reckless) and the
    <strong>self-serving bias</strong> (my wins are skill, my losses are the referee).
    Together they explain why eyewitness predictions of one's own obedience fail, why
    poverty is misattributed to laziness, and why situational redesign beats lectures on
    character.
  </p>

  <h2>Attitudes, persuasion and the dissonance loop</h2>
  <p>
    Attitudes have three components (cognition, affect, behaviour) and predict behaviour
    imperfectly — LaPierre's 1934 journey with a Chinese couple found near-universal
    courteous service and near-universal refusal by questionnaire months later: stated
    attitude and actual behaviour diverged. Persuasion theory explains when the gap
    closes: the <strong>elaboration likelihood model</strong> distinguishes central routes
    (argument quality, effortful — durable change) from peripheral routes (attractiveness,
    cue-driven — fast, fragile change); the same message persuades differently depending
    on the audience's motivation and ability to think. Attitude change also runs
    <em>backwards</em> from behaviour. <strong>Cognitive dissonance</strong> (Festinger,
    1957): acting against one's attitudes is aversive, and the mind relieves the tension
    by changing the attitude. The classic demonstration (Festinger & Carlsmith, 1959):
    participants paid one dollar to tell the next person a boring task was interesting later rated
    it as more enjoyable than those paid twenty dollars — the small payment was insufficient
    justification, so the attitude moved to close the gap. The same loop powers
    <strong>effort justification</strong> (Aronson & Mills: the harsher the initiation,
    the more group members value the group), the <strong>foot-in-the-door effect</strong>
    (comply with a small request, become the person who helps, comply with the large one),
    and post-decision dissonance (chosen options get revalued upward immediately after
    choosing).
  </p>

  <h2>Helping: the bystander effect and the subway test</h2>
  <p>
    Darley and Latané (1968) discovered <strong>diffusion of responsibility</strong>
    experimentally: hearing an apparent seizure over intercom, 85% of alone participants
    intervened; with others believed present, about 31%. Two mechanisms — responsibility
    divided among witnesses, and <strong>pluralistic ignorance</strong> (each witness
    reads the others' stillness as "probably nothing"). The press made the bystander
    effect famous with Kitty Genovese's 1964 murder, telling it as 38 indifferent
    neighbours; the better-reported truth is that several people did call out or call
    police, and the tale itself became a lesson in media simplification.
  </p>
  <p>
    The field experiment put the lab's feet on real tracks. Piliavin, Rodin and Piliavin
    (1969): a confederate staggers and collapses in a New York subway car — in some trials
    carrying a cane, in others smelling of liquor. Around 4,500 passengers watched. Findings
    that reshaped theory: <strong>helping was very high and fast</strong> in both
    conditions (the "apathetic city" myth dies on the platform); the cane victim was
    helped more often and faster than the drunk; helpers were overwhelmingly same-race in
    the drunk condition (a mild bias, weaker than expected); and — crucially —
    <strong>no diffusion of responsibility</strong>: helping did not fall as the car
    filled. The difference is anchoring: the victim collapsed at a specific passenger's
    feet, so responsibility was identifiable, not divided. Piliavin proposed a
    <strong>cost-reward model</strong> of bystander calculus — weigh helping costs (danger,
    embarrassment, delay) against not-helping costs (guilt, blame) — which predicts the
    cane-over-drunk asymmetry: the perceived cost of helping the drunk (and the cost of
    being wrong about danger) is higher. It remains the standard framework for designing
    interventions ("point at a specific person: you, call an ambulance").
  </p>

  <h2>Personal space: Perry and the physiology of distance</h2>
  <p>
    Personal space looks soft — a comfort, a custom — but it can be measured as hard data.
    Perry and colleagues (2013) ran two studies with autistic and non-autistic adults. The
    <strong>stop-distance paradigm</strong>: an experimenter walks toward the seated
    participant, who says when to halt; the distance at "stop" is the comfort boundary.
    Study two added physiology: skin conductance (electrodermal activity) recorded while a
    stranger approached and stood at varying distances. The results: autistic adults chose
    smaller comfortable distances with strangers, and — more tellingly — showed an
    <strong>altered coupling between distance and physiological arousal</strong>: the
    dose-response curve linking intrusion to bodily threat response was differently
    shaped, not just shifted. The methodological lesson is the social approach's own
    thesis in miniature: personal space is not a mere preference but an embodied,
    physiologically regulated system — which is why "just tolerate it" advice fails, and
    why the study informs how social environments for autistic people are designed
    (predictable distances, no surprise approaches).
  </p>
  <p>
    Two further concepts complete the toolkit. <strong>Stereotype threat</strong> (Steele
    & Aronson, 1995): making a negative group stereotype salient before a test — even by
    a demographic question — measurably depresses performance, via working-memory load
    and monitoring costs; it is a situational effect on ability expression, not an
    ability difference. <strong>The contact hypothesis</strong> (Allport, 1954):
    prejudice falls under contact — but with conditions, cooperation, common goals,
    equal status, institutional support. Pettigrew and Tropp's meta-analysis of over 500
    studies (2006) confirmed the overall negative relationship between contact and
    prejudice across contexts and cultures, with Allport's conditions strengthening it.
    It is one of social psychology's most policy-relevant victories.
  </p>

  <h2>Groups: presence, loafing, and the pathology of consensus</h2>
  <p>
    Mere presence changes performance. <strong>Social facilitation</strong> (Zajonc's
    1965 resolution of sixty contradictory studies): an audience strengthens the
    <em>dominant</em> response — better on well-learned tasks, worse on new ones
    (his demonstration extended even to cockroaches running faster along a runway
    with an audience of roaches). <strong>Social loafing</strong> runs the other
    way: in pooled tasks (rope-pulling, shouting in groups), individual effort
    drops as group size rises — responsibility diffuses, output falls — unless
    contributions are individually identifiable. And when the group deliberates,
    two distortions recur. <strong>Group polarisation</strong>: discussion amplifies
    the group's initial lean (deliberative juries of mild punitiveness become
    harsh ones) — the mechanism behind the echo-chamber worry, documented in the
    lab decades before social media. <strong>Groupthink</strong> (Janis, from the
    Bay of Pigs fiasco): cohesion plus isolation plus a directive leader produces
    illusion of invulnerability, self-censorship, and an illusion of unanimity —
    with the remedies correspondingly procedural: independent subgroups, devil's
    advocates, leaders speaking last. The pattern across all three: group
    membership is a technology that amplifies whatever the group starts with —
    including its errors.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The replication audit.</strong> Social psychology led its own crisis: several
    classic social-priming and elderly-walking-slowly effects shrank or failed on
    retest, while the big situational findings — conformity, obedience, dissonance,
    bystander costs and benefits, contact effects — held, often with mechanisms better
    specified than the originals. The durable lesson is about effect size and
    specification: situations are powerful when the situation is vivid and the pressure
    is real; weak verbal primes are not situations.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Beyond agency.</strong> Haslam and Reicher re-read Milgram: participants who
    obeyed were not passive automatons but <em>engaged followers</em> who believed in the
    science being served — obedience as identification with a cause, which fits the
    variations data (a second authority dissenting breaks the spell better than the
    victim's suffering does). Milgram's own notes support the reframing: he was troubled
    by how much his participants wanted to help "the experiment".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Does online contact reduce prejudice the way
    co-presence does (promising but thinner evidence)? How do norms propagate in
    networks — pluralistic ignorance seems far more durable in large online crowds,
    and "silent majority" miscalibration is measurable. And can situational design —
    defaults, anonymisation, designated responsibility — replace moral exhortation as
    the main lever of prosocial behaviour? The field's century says: bet on the
    situation.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Milgram (1963, 1974); Burger (2009); Asch (1951); Sherif (1935); Bond & Smith (1996); Festinger & Carlsmith (1959); Aronson & Mills (1959); Jones & Harris (1967); Darley & Latané (1968); Piliavin, Rodin & Piliavin (1969); Perry et al. (2013), PLoS ONE; Steele & Aronson (1995); Allport (1954); Pettigrew & Tropp (2006); Haslam & Reicher on engaged followership.</p>

